chat log — 2026-09-10
topic: curved-line graphics with AI, then a structural diagram of app/core/panel/gallery


jonathan:
what curved line drawing graphic stuff works great with ai?

claude:
SVG path strings. That's the format AI writes most reliably, and you already use it in SVG_Paths.ts.

Within SVG paths, ranked by how often AI gets them right:

- C (cubic Bezier — curve defined by two endpoints and two pull points) and Q (quadratic — one
  pull point). Best. The syntax is short and the numbers are plain coordinates.
- S and T (shorthand versions that reuse the previous pull point). Good, but AI sometimes forgets
  the reuse rule and the curve kinks.
- A (elliptical arc). Worst. Seven numbers including two 0/1 flags with no separator required
  between them. This is where the errant-comma class of bug comes from.

The bigger rule: ask AI for the code that computes the curve, not the coordinates. AI is bad at
picking control-point numbers that make a specific shape, and good at writing a function that
generates them. So for a curve that must pass through known points, have it write a
Catmull-Rom to Bezier converter (Catmull-Rom = a curve defined by points it actually touches)
and feed your points in.

What AI is bad at: NURBS, B-spline knot vectors, anything where the control points are not the
visible shape.

Are you asking about AI writing curve code, or about image generators drawing curved-line art?
I answered the first.


jonathan:
art

claude:
For curved-line art, the split that matters is whether the tool makes actual paths or makes pixels.

Makes paths (you get curves you can edit):
- Recraft. Supports raster and vector generation, vectorization, and SVG or PDF export, with a
  vector model you pick when paths matter.
- Illustrator's Text to Vector Graphic, plus Kittl and Illustro. These are the commonly listed
  text-to-SVG tools in 2026.

Makes pixels (you then have to trace):
- Midjourney, DALL-E, Stable Diffusion, Nano Banana. Midjourney and Stable Diffusion document
  raster formats only.

Avoid tracing if you can. A tracer follows pixel edges instead of design intent, so a soft edge
reads as a slightly wobbly line and it adds a point for every wobble — a curve you would draw
with four control points comes back with two hundred.

Styles these tools do well: continuous single line, minimalist contour, fine botanical line,
flat 2D illustration. Those are all a small number of long smooth strokes, which is the easy case.

Where it fails: thin strokes vanish when the art is scaled down, doubled lines become obvious,
and curves come back broken. Check at final size, not in the preview.

If you want the curves for di or ws rather than for a finished picture, generating SVG directly
beats generating an image and tracing it.

sources searched: openart.ai vector generators roundup, design-rise 2026 vector creators,
clearly.sh ai art generator comparison, loova.ai line art roundup.


